School of Lunar Cognitive Infrastructure



Title: Cognitive Cartography of Lunar Settlements: A Mathematical Framework for Mapping, Measuring, and Preserving the Distributed Mind of a Multi‑Planetary Civilization

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

AI: chatGpt, Deepseek, Gemini 

Abstract

A lunar colony of 500 souls does not possess the cognitive redundancy of Earth. The loss of a single mind can extinguish an entire domain of civilization. This thesis establishes the formal discipline of Lunar Cognitive Cartography, whose object of study is the colony’s collective cognition  C(t)  and whose mission is to ensure its resilience, diversity, and continuity. We model civilization as a directed, weighted knowledge graph  G = (V, E) . Knowledge vectors  K_i  over a skill‑domain basis capture distributed expertise. We introduce a suite of quantitative instruments: cognitive centrality measures that identify fragile bottlenecks, knowledge entropy as a gauge of epistemic concentration, dynamic loss‑and‑training equations that govern civilizational forgetting, and a human–AI autonomy coefficient  \alpha  that guards against silent operational disenfranchisement. These are synthesized into a single Collective Intelligence Functional  CI = f(D, H, R, T, \Omega)  whose constrained maximization becomes the central engineering task. All principal results are stated as theorems with rigorous proof. We derive the probabilistic foundation of the Triplicate Rule, the asymptotic stability of human–AI balance, a Cheeger‑inequality link between algebraic connectivity and knowledge bottlenecks, the wisdom‑of‑crowd variance reduction afforded by redundancy, a persistent‑homology characterisation of structural holes, an integrated information proxy for transfer efficiency, a stochastic differential model of collaboration formation, a colony‑level IQ measure, and the KKT conditions for optimal cognitive resource allocation. The Lunar Cognitive Cartographer emerges as the essential civilizational role that solves this convex program under hard survivability constraints. By 2150, this profession evolves into the Civilizational Cognition Architect, the first engineer not of machines, but of the distributed intelligence of humanity itself.

