Chapter 1: The Fragility of Lunar Knowing

A terrestrial civilization enjoys a vast, overlapping redundancy of expertise. On a frontier moon, we are stripped of that comfort. The historical records of early Antarctic bases and nuclear submarine crews demonstrate that isolated technical communities suffer cognitive cascade failures when key individuals are incapacitated. For a lunar settlement of five hundred, the map of knowledge is sparse and perilous. There may exist only one person who truly understands the regolith sinterer, only one who holds the constitutional precedent for emergency powers. Their loss is not merely a human tragedy; it is the excision of a civilizational organ.

This thesis posits that such fragility is not inevitable. It can be measured, modeled, and mitigated. To do so, we must elevate cognition from a private possession to a colony‑scale resource, subject to the same rigorous management as oxygen or water. We build the mathematical architecture for this task.

