Chapter 2: The Cognitive Network Model

Definition 2.1 (Cognitive Graph). Let a lunar settlement be modeled by a finite weighted directed graph  G = (V, E)  where

V = V_H \cup V_A \cup V_I
\]  

is a disjoint union of human nodes, AI nodes, and institutional/database nodes. For  i, j \in V , edge weight  w_{ij} \in [0,1]  quantifies normalized knowledge‑transfer capacity from  i  to  j . The adjacency matrix  A  satisfies  A_{ij} = w_{ij} .

Definition 2.2 (Knowledge Vector Space). Fix an orthonormal basis of  n  independent skill domains  \{s_1,\dots,s_n\} : medicine, robotics, ecology, law, governance, etc. Each node  i  possesses a knowledge vector

K_i = (k_{i1}, k_{i2}, \dots, k_{in})
\]  

with  k_{is} \in [0,1]  measuring verified competence in domain  s . The total colony knowledge in that domain is the envelope of available expertise:

K_{s, \text{colony}} = \max_{i \in V} k_{is}.
\]  

The colony’s knowledge state is the union vector  K_{\text{colony}} = (K_{s, \text{colony}})_{s=1}^n .

Lemma 2.1 (Monotonicity).  K_{\text{colony}}  is monotone under addition of nodes: if  V' = V \cup \{j\} , then  K'_{s, \text{colony}} \ge K_{s, \text{colony}}  for all  s .
Proof. Immediate from the maximum operation. ∎

The cognitive graph becomes the fundamental object of diagnosis. Its structure reveals not just who knows what, but how knowledge flows—and where it pools dangerously.

