Chapter 3: Cognitive Centrality and the Identification of Bottlenecks

A person who knows things is valuable; a person through whom knowledge must flow is a structural vulnerability.

Definition 3.1 (Degree and Betweenness Centrality).
Degree centrality measures direct connectivity:

D_i = \sum_j A_{ij}.
\]  

Betweenness centrality quantifies how often a node lies on the shortest knowledge‑diffusion paths between others:

B_i = \sum_{s \neq t \neq i} \frac{\sigma_{st}(i)}{\sigma_{st}},
\]  

where  \sigma_{st}  is the total number of shortest paths from  s  to  t , and  \sigma_{st}(i)  counts those passing through  i .

Theorem 3.1 (Bottleneck Impact). The removal of node  i^* = \arg\max_i B_i  maximizes the increase in the graph’s average shortest‑path length  \ell(G) .
Proof. By definition,  B_i  measures the fraction of all geodesics that depend on  i . Removing  i^*  eliminates the greatest number of geodesics, forcing the maximum amount of rerouting and thus the maximal elongation of mean path length. ∎

Such an individual becomes a civilizational single point of failure. A Cognitive Cartographer’s first task is to identify all nodes with high betweenness and ensure their knowledge is not a unique conduit.

