Chapter 8: The Cognitive Digital Twin

Prediction requires a living model.

Definition 8.1 (Cognitive Digital Twin State). The twin is a dynamical system

X_c(t) = \{K_i(t)\}_{i \in V}, \{\gamma_s(t)\}_{s=1}^n, \{R_s(t)\}, \alpha(t), G(t)
\]  

that mirrors the colony’s expertise map, institutional memory, and training pipelines. Its evolution is given by  \dot{X}_c = \mathcal{F}(X_c, u)  where  u(t)  encodes training interventions.

A linearized prediction operator  \mathcal{P}_\tau  forecasts future states:

X_c(t+\tau) = e^{\mathcal{L}\tau} X_c(t) + \int_0^\tau e^{\mathcal{L}(\tau-s)} u(s) ds.
\]  

The Cartographer uses this to identify looming shortages and test mitigation strategies in silico before human lives depend on them. The digital twin also integrates real‑time measures such as the integrated information proxy introduced later (Lemma 10.4).

