10.3 The Cost Structure of Cognitive Engineering

In a lunar settlement, the “budget” for training is not a simple linear sum of time. It is an aggregation of metabolic energy, oxygen overhead, cognitive fatigue, and AI‑support load. We formalise this with a convex cost function.

Definition 10.3 (Cost Function for Expertise Acquisition).
The total resource cost to bring node  i  to proficiency  k_{is}  in domain  s  is

\mathcal{C}_{is}(k_{is}) = \int_{0}^{k_{is}} \Big[ \Phi_s(\tau) \; \Psi_i(\tau) \; \Xi_i(\tau) \Big] d\tau,
\]  

where

· \Phi_s(\tau) : domain difficulty – intrinsic complexity of skill  s ,
· \Psi_i(\tau) : individual aptitude – metabolic and neuro‑plastic cost for person  i ,
· \Xi_i(\tau) : infrastructure overhead – AI‑assistance and habitat resources required during training.

All three factors are strictly positive and increasing, so \mathcal{C}_{is} is strictly convex and monotonically increasing.

The marginal cost (the gradient used in optimisation) is the derivative

c_{i,s}(k_{is}) \equiv \frac{\partial \mathcal{C}_{is}}{\partial k_{is}} = \Phi_s(k_{is})\; \Psi_i(k_{is})\; \Xi_i(k_{is}) > 0.

---

10.4 The Optimal Allocation Equilibrium

Equipped with the true cost of training, we solve the Cartographer’s primal problem:

\max_{\{k_{is}\}} CI(\mathbf{k}) \quad \text{subject to} \quad \sum_{i,s} \mathcal{C}_{is}(k_{is}) \le C_{\text{total}},
\]  

where C_{\text{total}} is the colony’s total cognitive resource budget.

Theorem 10.4 (Optimal Allocation Equilibrium).
If \nabla^2 CI is negative definite (concave) and the cost functions \mathcal{C}_{is} are strictly convex, then a unique global maximum exists, and the optimal competences \{k_{is}^*\} satisfy the Karush–Kuhn–Tucker conditions:

\frac{\partial CI}{\partial k_{is}} = \mu \, c_{i,s}(k_{is}), \qquad \mu \ge 0,
\]  

where \mu is the Lagrange multiplier (shadow price) of the total budget.

Proof.

1. Convexity: The feasible set defined by \sum \mathcal{C}_{is}(k_{is}) \le C_{\text{total}} is convex because each \mathcal{C}_{is} is convex. The objective CI is concave (Theorem 10.1). Slater’s condition holds for any positive budget.
2. Lagrangian:

\mathcal{L}(\mathbf{k}, \mu) = CI(\mathbf{k}) - \mu \left( \sum_{i,s} \mathcal{C}_{is}(k_{is}) - C_{\text{total}} \right).
\]  

3. Stationarity:

\frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}{\partial k_{is}} = \frac{\partial CI}{\partial k_{is}} - \mu \frac{\partial \mathcal{C}_{is}}{\partial k_{is}} = 0
\quad \Longrightarrow \quad \frac{\partial CI}{\partial k_{is}} = \mu \, c_{i,s}(k_{is}).
\]  

4. Uniqueness: CI is strictly concave in the active variables and the constraint set is strictly convex; thus the solution is unique. ∎

Corollary 10.4.1 (Golden Rule of Training).
At the optimum, the marginal collective intelligence gain per unit of resource is equalised across all skills and individuals:

\frac{1}{c_{i,s}} \frac{\partial CI}{\partial k_{is}} = \mu \quad \text{for all } i,s \text{ with } k_{is} > 0.

This prescribes exactly where the Cartographer should invest the next hour of training time or the next kilocalorie of metabolic budget.

